          "name": "Venkata Ravi Theja Reddy Venuthurla",
          "email": "venkata.ravi.theja.reddy.venu@oracle.com",
          "date": "2026-09-12 00:03:03.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20680 socklnd: order delayed receive state publication",
        "message": "LU-20680 socklnd: order delayed receive state publication\n\nlnet_parse() may call ksocknal_recv() asynchronously while\nksocknal_process_receive() waits for it to publish the receive tuple.\n\nksocknal_recv() initializes ksnc_lnet_msg, ksnc_rx_nob_left,\nksnc_rx_discard, and ksnc_rx_to before changing ksnc_rx_state to\nSOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD.  However, the scheduler reads ksnc_rx_state\nwithout holding kss_lock.\n\nOn weakly ordered CPUs, the scheduler can observe\nSOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD before the associated receive tuple is visible.\nSince an empty iov_iter is valid, it treats the payload as already\nreceived and skips ksocknal_receive().  It then finalizes the NULL\nmessage pointer on the first message, or the stale already-finalized\nmessage pointer on a subsequent message.  ksocknal_new_packet() then\nstarts reading a Ksock header from payload bytes which remain unread in\nthe socket.\n\nPublish SOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD with release semantics and consume it\nwith acquire semantics.  This ensures that observing the payload state\nalso makes the complete receive tuple visible to the scheduler.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Venkata Ravi Theja Reddy Venuthurla <venkata.ravi.theja.reddy.venu@oracle.com>\nChange-Id: Idf677a496ba656e850aca754b20b1ec57fda6024\n"
      },
